  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nLargely pleasant conditions continue through the end of the work\nweek and into the first half of the weekend despite a series of\nfronts approaching the region this afternoon. A low pressure\nsystem crossing the James Bay is currently lifting a warm front\nthrough central New York, identifiable on visible satellite as a\nband of clouds approaching the western Adirondacks. As this is a\nvery moisture-starved system, little precipitation is ongoing\nand is likewise expected as a result of this initial passage.\nHowever, the ensuing cold front, which will cross through the\nregion overnight, should bring some additional forcing and\nmoisture advection off the Great Lakes to force orographically\nenhanced showers in the Adirondacks overnight. A few of these\nshowers could drift as far south and east as the Southern Greens\nand Taconics, again aided by orographic lift to overcome low-\nlevel dry air, but resulting rainfall amounts will not be\nappreciable. \n\nCyclonic flow beneath an upper-level trough associated with the\nlow could favor lingering light sprinkles or showers in the \nAdirondacks tomorrow, but elsewhere conditions will remain dry. \nIn the wake of the warm front, temperatures will moderate \nbriefly near to even slightly above normal levels, though the \nsteepened pressure gradient and sufficient mixing will maintain \nbreezy conditions. Courtesy of the cold frontal passage,\ntemperatures Friday will drop back to normal or even slightly\nbelow, still with breezy winds as high pressure begins to build\nin and all lingering precipitation ceases. By Saturday, winds\nwill slacken and temperatures will rise by a few degrees, making\nit the perfect day for fall activities. \n\nThe next best chance for beneficial, potentially widespread\nprecipitation comes Sunday afternoon into Monday when a low\npressure system ejects east-northeastward out of the Midwest. At\nthis time, there continues to be decent agreement in some areas\nseeing rain by Sunday afternoon, though the duration and amounts\nare uncertain. The dependent variable driving those outcomes \nare the track of the system which currently shows considerable \nspread in the medium- to long-range ensembles. That said, there\nis a very low probability in flooding impacts as a result of the\nrainfall we receive from this system. The latest NBM 24 hr QMD\nprobabilities for at least 1\" of rain range from about 10-40%\nacross our area. Considering we are in need of rain, and\ncertainly will be by Sunday, even an inch would not be\nsufficient to drive flooding across any part of our area.",
